ion of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(2) & =0.05+0.25 \\\\  & =0.30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, we have that $f(2,3)=0.25.$ \n\nFinally, substituting ${f}_{X}(2)=0.30$ and $f(2,3)=0.25$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y=3\\,|\\,X=2) & =\\frac{f(2,3)}{{f}_{X}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.25}{0.30} \\\\  & ≈0.83.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534596 17592186045417 true] [17592186162374 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534596 17592186045417 true] [17592186162374 1071 "| $f$ | $Y = 0$ | $Y = 1$ | $Y = 2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X = 0$ | $0.1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $X = 1$ | $0$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X = 2$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n\nFind the conditional probability mass function $f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid 2)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x, y)$ shown in the table above.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139534596 17592186045417 false] [17592186162374 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n\nFind the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139534596 17592186045417 true] [17592186162374 1074 "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the following set of conditional probabilities:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=2)\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=y\\,|\\,X=2)=\\frac{P(X=2,Y=y)}{P(X=2)}=\\frac{f(2,y)}{{f}_{X}(2)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distributions for $X$ and $Y$ correspond to the row and column totals, respectively. So, let's compute these:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.6$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ | $0.4$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, for $y\\in \\{0,1,2\\},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y=0\\,|\\,X=2) & =\\frac{f(2,0)}{{f}_{X}(2)}=\\frac{0.1}{0.6}=\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ P(Y=1\\,|\\,X=2) & =\\frac{f(2,1)}{{f}_{X}(2)}=\\frac{0.2}{0.6}=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ P(Y=2\\,|\\,X=2) & =\\frac{f(2,2)}{{f}_{X}(2)}=\\frac{0.3}{0.6}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the conditional probability mass function can be represented by the following table:   \n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |" 13194139534596 17592186045417 true] [17592186163288 1064 "Let's recall the multiplication law for conditional probability for two events ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) and ![](images/5d/5d8b650b90c85fbc0db5e4b6fae61767e293a843d18a2526f3ee4157733e1f4b.svg)\n![](images/44/44229a2a35108e36c1348009a552be50e798af9b2807f49c4e4ef8515ed7eefe.svg)\n\nNow suppose that ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) and ![](images/ab/ab4817775494786b06f0b9cecf225ea5979c035b69c36c1fa078f835524be9e9.svg) are discrete random variables. The conditional probability that ![](images/2c/2cc57b1c8b00862c078193ea1774f4012e1513ab1a26a6d5bb806e046220a812.svg) *given that* ![](images/46/46851627a248368806191a89ed2bb59d72b83b2e352f768da26f41305e156052.svg) follows immediately from the multiplication law and is given by\n\n![](images/2b/2b705d0acedbb080625148287647d075dce75f40531615a52352a848a85892fe.svg)\n\nwhere ![](images/a2/a2d