c7.svg)\n\nIn a similar manner, the conditional probability that ![](images/46/46851627a248368806191a89ed2bb59d72b83b2e352f768da26f41305e156052.svg) given that ![](images/2c/2cc57b1c8b00862c078193ea1774f4012e1513ab1a26a6d5bb806e046220a812.svg) can be calculated as ![](images/8c/8c8bdddcd02a672d59ded44b5281cf2f24236f88b2f494730bac998a760e9b0a.svg)\n\nwhere ![](images/fd/fdb49440ceb7e11cd9ba0b0869a1e6d3565549969180830d1796db7dd00a2b96.svg) is the marginal mass function of ![](images/ed/ed6dc1fd057e3dd2207c735a562bf4db22b72d1a9380651607439f00c0440725.svg) This formula assumes that ![](images/f8/f8020df80e303daf760cd9bf4d8437cc08bc4b95b0ccf64bc7a43ccddc650f5f.svg)" 13194139534596 17592186045417 false] [17592186163325 1064 "We can now define two more probability mass functions as follows:\n\n- The **conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$,** denoted $f_{X|Y}(x|y),$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_{X|Y}(x|y) = P(X = x \\,|\\, Y = y) = \\dfrac{P(X = x, \\, Y = y)}{P(Y=y)} = \\dfrac{f(x,y)}{f_Y(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere  $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and $f_Y(y)$ is the marginal mass function of $Y.$\n\n- The **conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x,$** denoted $f_{Y|X}(y|x),$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_{Y|X}(y|x) = P(Y = y \\,|\\, X = x) = \\dfrac{P(X = x,\\, Y = y)}{P(X=x)} = \\dfrac{f(x,y)}{f_X(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere  $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and $f_X(x)$ is the marginal mass function of $X.$\n\n**Watch out!** The conditional distribution of $X$ given $Y$ does *not* equal the conditional distribution of $Y$ given $X$. That is, in general,\n\n$$\nf_{X|Y}(x|y)  \\ne f_{Y|X}(y|x).\n$$\n\nFinally, if $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then \n\n$$\nf_{X|Y}(x|y)=f_X(x),\\qquad f_{Y|X}(y|x)=f_Y(y).\n$$\n\nIn other words, if $X$  and  $Y$ are independent, then knowing the value of one random variable does not affect the probability distribution of the other." 13194139534596 17592186045417 true] [17592186163325 1064 "We can now define two more probability mass functions as follows:\n\n- The **conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$,** denoted $f_{X|Y}(x|y),$ is defined as $$f_{X|Y}(x|y) = P(X = x \\,|\\, Y = y) = \\dfrac{P(X = x, \\, Y = y)}{P(Y=y)} = \\dfrac{f(x,y)}{f_Y(y)},$$ where $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and $f_Y(y)$ is the marginal mass function of $Y.$\n\n- The **conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x,$** denoted $f_{Y|X}(y|x),$ is defined as $$f_{Y|X}(y|x) = P(Y = y \\,|\\, X = x) = \\dfrac{P(X = x,\\, Y = y)}{P(X=x)} = \\dfrac{f(x,y)}{f_X(x)},$$ where $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and $f_X(x)$ is the marginal mass function of $X.$\n\n**Watch out!** The conditional distribution of $X$ given $Y$ does *not* equal the conditional distribution of $Y$ given $X$. That is, in general,\n\n$$f_{X|Y}(x|y) \\ne f_{Y|X}(y|x).$$\n\nFinally, if $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then\n\n$$f_{X|Y}(x|y)=f_X(x),\\qquad f_{Y|X}(y|x)=f_Y(y).$$\n\nIn other words, if $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then knowing the value of one random variable does not affect the probability distribution of the other." 13194139534596 17592186045417 false] [17592186427178 1080 "| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |" 13194139534596 17592186045417 true] [17592186427178 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534596 17592186045417 true] [17592186427178 1083 #uuid "02d64482-a157-593b-a0d1-db5b6744fac2" 13194139534596 17592186045417 true] [17592186427179 1080 "| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |" 13194139534596 17592186045417 true] [17592186427179 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534596 17592186045417 true] [17592186427179 1083 #uuid "06f6b37e-e1c4-56fe-af0d-d05220252493" 13194139534596 17592186045417 tr